    <!--
        The keys of the on-screen letter picker: the alphabet of the language, then the digits. Each character once. Nine keys to a
        row; a language with more than 54 keys gets more, slightly smaller keys so that six rows are enough (Czech has 51).
        The search compares letters as typed, so a language whose titles are not written in its picker letters says how here:
        Japanese: the 46 basic hiragana in gojuon order, then the digits. Katakana titles are found through hiragana (the search
        folds katakana to hiragana and ignores dakuten, small kana and the long-vowel mark) and kanji titles through a built-in
        table of readings.
        Korean: the 14 initial consonants (choseong) ㄱ ㄴ ㄷ ㄹ ㅁ ㅂ ㅅ ㅇ ㅈ ㅊ ㅋ ㅌ ㅍ ㅎ, then A-Z and the digits. A query of them finds
        the titles whose syllables start with those consonants in that order (ㅎㅅㄷ finds 해상도).
        Simplified Chinese: A-Z and the digits. Besides Latin text, the letters match the pinyin initials of the title (sz finds 设置).
    -->
    <string name="settings_screen_search_letters">ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789</string>
